{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact registration-recovery head d1080a525b4e3a0973b9188dacd12fe833f0c48c on builder/oauth-registration-recovery-20261103, tree 2fad1148, for delivery request b951e179 under promise 304c4034 and review request 57600020. b951e179 carries request c37ba98f and promise e597584a, because c37ba98f declared by mistake that it owes no Git artifact. This approval covers registration expiry, reclamation, the legacy migration and grant retention. It settles my finding 35b3a7a0 as written. It claims no deployment and no O4 acceptance.\n\nScope. Main 59232978 is an ancestor, and the worktree is clean. d1080a52 differs from 4251ef12, which I reviewed in full, only by the note's 12-line delivery correction. Against main, the head changes hosted.ts, oauth-clients.ts, oauth-issuer.ts, oauth-parameters.ts, the scope Worker, their tests, the security inventory test, docs/mcp.md, the design, the note and four manifests. The commit-message check passes for all seven commits. No card path changes. From the gate commit 41a4a7e9 to the head, only the note, the flush note and the manifests change. Page run 41607711026 succeeded at 41a4a7e9.\n\nWhat holds:\n\n- register deletes every expired row, counts the rest and inserts the new row in one SQL transaction. A new row expires one hour after registration. Client 1,001 therefore succeeds as soon as one unused row expires, and the capacity case shows it.\n- resolve refuses an expired registration and never writes. The anonymous-renewal control renews on lookup, and the capacity case fails.\n- Only the agent object calls retain, through McpRegistry.retainClient. No Worker route reaches it. retain requires a live row whose stored text equals the resolved client exactly. It extends the row to at most 30 days from now and never shortens it.\n- #grant calls retention only after the signed control and fresh Native authority pass. After the await, it rereads the agent row, reruns #grantAuthority and rechecks the signed deadline. The held-reply case widens the agent's actions during the wait. The grant then refuses with 403 and stores no issuer row.\n- issue refuses a registration deadline shorter than one code lifetime and caps the grant's end at that deadline. Refresh and access tokens therefore never outlive the registration, and the issuer case shows access expiry at the cap. A replayed operation returns its saved answer and renews nothing.\n- A pre-upgrade table gains the expires column and one deadline 30 days out, in one transaction. A rebuilt registry keeps that deadline. No deployed Worker holds such a table, because O3 never deployed.\n\nThe gate. Ruling d01d06a5 asks me to judge the sealed record, and it is enough for this change. gate.exit at 41a4a7e9 reads exit 1, and the gate stays failed on the record. My reasons:\n\n- Every stage's cases pass, Native included: 1,061 with two skips. The three owning files are Native files, so the gate itself ran and passed every new case on the frozen source.\n- The one failure is the same EnvironmentTeardownError, attributed to the same packages/scope/test/archive-input.test.ts as at the flush gate. That gate held no registration change, so this race exists without this head.\n- source-before and source-after both record 41a4a7e9, tree 94f916e1 and a clean status. The named file alone passes with exit 0.\n\nEvidence. The four manifests hash to deb52e37…, cd455978…, cb323bc8… and a8f5d6a2…, and the note names all four. All 34 public files match their sizes and digests and are read-only, every SHA256SUMS file matches, and no unit holds an unlisted file. All 53 private files match their indexes. Both controls name baseline 6e5e64bc…, the exact oauth-clients.ts at the head, and each exits 1 at its intended marker. The scan for private keys, GitHub tokens and bearer values finds no match.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The registry still owns client records, and only a Native-authorized grant extends one.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding. Two limits remain, and the note states both:\n\n1. Anonymous callers can still fill the table again every hour. 1,000 requests per hour keep new registrations refused. Recovery now needs no operator, but the denial can be sustained cheaply. When the planner deploys O4, a rate limit on POST /oauth/register at the edge would close this, or the planner can accept the risk by name.\n2. A member of any room on the Worker can protect up to 1,000 registered clients for 30 days by consenting to each. Native authority records every such consent, so the member stays accountable.\n\nSimplification: no required change.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:16d4bcf17e223194b265c810a331c1b9265345b9","binding":"assigned","head":"d1080a525b4e3a0973b9188dacd12fe833f0c48c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:f8c336638271caadcf9bc267d3844c1ea81f5cae\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:304c403439631bb8922235ee2431069a78bd47da\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:1bb63a2f2b09f5caf0fa6b3d59f3c6344e20d9d3","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"approved"}}